    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-5998",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula",
      "problem": "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,4].$",
      "worked_solution": "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+\\frac{1}{8}(4)^{2}]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
