      "math_academy_id": "q-162161",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment",
      "problem": "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(3,0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+1+0=4.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
