      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Row by a Number",
      "problem": "Suppose $X$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(X)=-60.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{4}:=-\\frac{4}{15}{R}_{4}.$ What is the value of $det(Y)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The row operation applied to $X$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{4}{15}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-\\frac{4}{15}det(X) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{15}\\cdot (-60) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
