    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-130740",
      "knowledge_point_id": "272d9a54-ba97-5175-86df-0a78337da7b9",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Matrix by a Scalar",
      "problem": "Suppose $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $det(B)=-4.$ What is the value of $det(5B)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot B)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=5$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(5B) & ={5}^{2}\\cdot det(B) \\\\  & =25\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-100.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
