  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082528",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-130743",
      "knowledge_point_id": "24e5e193-5e30-5480-9e5d-b7b8f1d5de87",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Determinant After Applying Several Elementary Operations",
      "problem": "Suppose $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(U)=21.$ The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(V)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{4}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{3},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{3}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(V) & =\\frac{1}{3}det(U) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
